    <h1>Mandatory Roth Catch-Up for High Earners ($155,000 Rule)</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">
      <strong>Short answer:</strong> Under SECURE 2.0 Section 603 (final regulations published September 16, 2025, effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2026), if your 2026 FICA wages (W-2 Box 3) from the employer sponsoring your plan exceeded <strong>$155,000</strong>, your 2027 catch-up contributions must be Roth.
    </p>

    <div class="cta-back">
      <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/" style="text-decoration: none;">Check Your Roth Split in the Calculator →</a>
    </div>

    <h2>Key Details of the $155,000 Threshold</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Which wages count:</strong> Prior-year (2026) FICA wages shown in <strong>W-2 Box 3</strong> from the employer sponsoring the 401(k) plan—not total household income and not wages from an unrelated prior employer.</li>
      <li><strong>No FICA wages:</strong> If you had no FICA wages from that employer in 2026 (for example, certain self-employed or governmental employees not subject to Social Security tax), the mandatory Roth rule does not apply.</li>
      <li><strong>Base $25,500 is still your choice:</strong> The mandatory Roth requirement applies only to the catch-up portion above $25,500 ($8,500 for 50–59/64+, or $11,750 for 60–63). You can still make the first $25,500 pre-tax.</li>
      <li><strong>If your plan does not offer Roth:</strong> High earners subject to the $155,000 rule cannot make catch-up contributions unless the plan adds a Roth option.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

